**user**:
> I have a dataset of GDP per capita and population, with columns `Country Code`, year, gdppc, pop. Can you create some R logic to make a Lorenz curve (i.e., gini coefficient graph)?
**assistant**:
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> library(ggplot2)
> 
> # Lorenz curve and population-weighted Gini for one year
> make_lorenz <- function(data, selected_year) {
> 
>   lorenz <- data %>%
>     filter(year == selected_year,
>            !is.na(gdppc), !is.na(pop),
>            gdppc >= 0, pop > 0) %>%
>     arrange(gdppc) %>%
>     mutate(
>       total_gdp = gdppc * pop,
>       cum_pop_share = cumsum(pop) / sum(pop),
>       cum_gdp_share = cumsum(total_gdp) / sum(total_gdp)
>     ) %>%
>     # Add origin, required for correct integration/plotting
>     bind_rows(tibble(
>       `Country Code` = NA_character_,
>       year = selected_year,
>       gdppc = NA_real_,
>       pop = 0,
>       total_gdp = 0,
>       cum_pop_share = 0,
>       cum_gdp_share = 0
>     )) %>%
>     arrange(cum_pop_share)
> 
>   # Gini = 1 - 2 * area beneath Lorenz curve
>   gini <- lorenz %>%
>     summarise(
>       gini = 1 - sum(
>         diff(cum_pop_share) *
>           (head(cum_gdp_share, -1) + tail(cum_gdp_share, -1))
>       )
>     ) %>%
>     pull(gini)
> 
>   p <- ggplot(lorenz, aes(cum_pop_share, cum_gdp_share)) +
>     geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = "dashed",
>                 color = "grey50") +
>     geom_line(linewidth = 1.1, color = "#0072B2") +
>     coord_equal() +
>     labs(
>       title = paste0("Global GDP per-capita Lorenz Curve, ", selected_year),
>       subtitle = paste0("Population-weighted Gini coefficient = ",
>                         round(gini, 3)),
>       x = "Cumulative share of world population",
>       y = "Cumulative share of world GDP"
>     ) +
>     theme_minimal()
> 
>   list(lorenz = lorenz, gini = gini, plot = p)
> }
> 
> # Example
> result <- make_lorenz(df, selected_year = 2023)
> 
> result$gini
> result$plot
> ```
> 
> This is a **population-weighted international inequality** measure: countries are ordered by `gdppc`, while their population determines each country’s weight. Total GDP is calculated as `gdppc * pop`.
